(*
Issue #550's own fix (powerStackNearlyExhausted, ParseExpr.cpp) adds a
live stack-headroom check to Parser::parsePower's right-associative
recursion, which had no ceiling at all before. That check MUST NOT fire
before Sema's own, pre-existing MaxExprDepth=1000 (Sema.h) would reject the
same input anyway -- a 1,000-term '**' chain is exactly at that boundary,
using only a small fraction of the stack headroom the parser-level check
budgets against (see that comment for the arithmetic), so this should
compile exactly as far as it did on main before this fix existed: parsed
in full, then cleanly rejected by Sema, at the same column every time
(":5006:" is 1000 terms' worth of "2 ** " at 5 characters each, plus the
leading "const x = "). A shift in that column would mean either guard's
threshold moved in a way that changes where the FIRST one to fire now
sits, which is itself worth noticing.
*)

(*
RUN: not %plang -std=iso10206 %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: :5006: error: expression is nested too deeply
*)

